the largest tv news network in the  philippines has had to lay off hundreds  of workers and massively curtail news  operations  after lawmakers voted to reject its  license renewal  abs-cbn has shuttered 53 of its regional  television and radio stations  leaving only its cable operations in the  capital manila  untouched the network had in the past  been highly critical of president  rodrigo duterte  and his so-called war on drugs the  curtailment of its operations  has been described as a black day for  independent media  in the country  a death knell for media freedom in the  philippines  for decades abs-cbn  broadcast vital information to isolated  communities  through its regional network now it has  been forced to slash its operations  leaving millions of filipinos without a  crucial source of local news  there will be many filipinos in rural  provinces  who will no longer have access to  accurate information  and that's where the issue of press  freedom comes in  laid off workers and members of the  public gathered outside the broadcaster  in manila  to mourn the loss of the regional  stations  they also added their names to a  petition launched by a group of citizen  volunteers  the group hopes to collect seven million  signatures  and push congress to renew the broadcast  franchise  i grew up watching abs abs-cbn as a  child  i love this network their programs keep  me company  and entertain me when i come home from  work tired  it makes me cry when i think about  abs-cbn not being allowed to operate  [Music]  actually i feel a mix of emotions  it will be hypocritical to say that we  are not angry with those who killed our  franchise  but we believe that the day will come  that we will be allowed to be back  on air again  the media freedom watchdog reporters  without borders says journalism in the  philippines has been under increasing  attack  since president rodrigo duterte came to  power  abs cbn is one of the most high-profile  casualties  maria resa is editor-in-chief of the  news site rappler  and knows a thing or two about earning  the displeasure of the duterte  government he joins me now  on the line from manila maria i'd like  to  uh ask you first about abs-cbn  place it in context for us what does it  mean for the average filipino  it is the largest broadcaster in the  philippines  it's radio stations reach parts of the  philippines that no other  news group hits it is also known as the  star network its entertainment group is  huge uh it's the star network right  so news entertainment uh it is a company  that employs  11 000 people uh and to have this  uh shut down is a huge blow and  definitely meant to be a cautionary tale  for journalists  where does this leave independent media  in the philippines maria  an endangered species you know uh all of  the cases against me and rappler  in 2018 there were 11 filed by the  philippine government  uh and then the arrest warrants that  have gone through the can  one conviction on june 15th this year  all of this is just  one journalist and one small group right  it was a cautionary tale  but to shut down abs-cbn is  unthinkable before the time of covid in  fact the last time it happened was in  1972  when ferdinand marcos declared martial  law and the network was shut down for 14  years  you know so is this bringing forward  another  decade or so of a dictatorship  this is a question in the minds of  filipinos and frankly  for journalists to shut shutdown abs-cbn  is tremendous power  this is what we're up against what do  you think  maria are things going to get far worse  under the detector government  i think it already has if you look at  what coven 19 has done right  we have had the longest lockdown this is  the 24th  week of a lockdown that is largely  driven by  security forces and we have incidents of  kovitz still exponentially  increasing meantime as early as april 1  you have president duterte  telling security forces to shoot anyone  who violates  quarantine rules this is uh not a good  way to deal with that  having said that in the middle of the  pandemic on may 5th  a small regulatory group issued this  order to shut down abs-cbn  our house of representatives a working  committee there then took away the  franchise voted against it  so you can see that we have at least a  captured legislature  it is we're on the precipice fighting  for our democracy  maria risa we'll have to leave it there  for the time being but thank you so much  for joining us  you
 

